WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.465
While a population has both
GPOs and Intune policies,

2
00:00:03.465 --> 00:00:08.200
three conflict systems are in play,
and the program plans against each one.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.843
Inside Intune: two compliance policies
take the most restrictive value;

4
00:00:12.843 --> 00:00:17.408
a compliance setting beats the same
setting in a configuration policy;

5
00:00:17.408 --> 00:00:21.664
and between configuration policies
of any type, settings catalog,

6
00:00:21.664 --> 00:00:25.688
endpoint security, security
baselines, there is no precedence.

7
00:00:25.688 --> 00:00:29.850
The conflict is reported and the setting
might not apply until it's fixed.

8
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.784
The chapter states two corrections
to earlier program material:

9
00:00:33.784 --> 00:00:36.261
profiles don't resolve by last write wins,

10
00:00:36.261 --> 00:00:40.850
and endpoint security doesn't
automatically beat configuration profiles.

11
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:44.050
So each setting has
exactly one owning policy.

12
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.642
Between Group Policy and
MDM: in the Policy CSP,

13
00:00:47.642 --> 00:00:53.598
Group Policy keeps winning unless MDM wins
over GP is set to one; set it where a

14
00:00:53.598 --> 00:00:59.837
population is in transition and remove
the GPO copy as Intune takes the setting.

15
00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:01.979
Outside the Policy CSP it's a race,

16
00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:06.899
and Windows Hello for Business shouldn't
be configured through both at all.

17
00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:12.748
For Windows Update, Microsoft
recommends Ignore Windows Update

18
00:01:12.748 --> 00:01:18.812
Group Policies instead, on Windows
11 24H2 with KB5101684 and later.

19
00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:21.464
Between Configuration Manager and Intune,

20
00:01:21.464 --> 00:01:25.000
the co-management slider sets
one authority per workload.

21
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.091
Note two traps: switching device
configuration also moves resource access and

22
00:01:30.091 --> 00:01:33.485
Endpoint Protection; and
with Pilot Intune selected,

23
00:01:33.485 --> 00:01:37.650
Intune deploys policies but doesn't
remove them on unassignment.

24
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.038
Disabling a link is the moment production
behavior changes, so it's gated.

25
00:01:42.038 --> 00:01:46.872
Freeze the GPO, back it up, prove
the replacement reports success,

26
00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:51.525
then disable the link, not the
GPO, with one reversible command.

27
00:01:51.525 --> 00:01:54.135
And remember that preference
items stay on the

28
00:01:54.135 --> 00:01:56.880
device unless configured
to remove themselves.
